        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What deck length do I need to run on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Sixty inches is the working minimum for anyone over about 5'8", and we would treat it as non-negotiable above 6 feet or above 8 mph. Fifty-five-inch decks are adequate for walking and jogging and become a real constraint once your stride extends, because you shorten your gait to stay clear of the rear roller. Deck length is the specification we would compromise on last.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much horsepower does a treadmill need for running?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Three continuous-duty horsepower (3.0 CHP) is sufficient for almost any home runner, and above that the returns are small. Ignore peak horsepower figures entirely — they describe a burst the motor cannot sustain and exist mainly for the box. A 3.0 CHP machine with a 60-inch deck is a better running treadmill than a 4.0 CHP machine with a 55-inch one, every time.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is running on a treadmill worse for your knees than running outside?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No — if anything the cushioned deck reduces peak impact compared with tarmac. The more common treadmill-specific complaints are calf and Achilles related, and they usually come from a shortened stride on a deck that is too small or from an over-soft surface that absorbs the elastic return your lower leg is meant to get back. A correctly sized, firm deck largely removes both.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Should I set the treadmill to 1% incline to simulate outdoor running?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The 1% convention comes from a 1996 study and applies mainly at faster paces, roughly 7 mph and above, where air resistance outdoors becomes significant. At easier paces the difference is small enough to ignore. Setting 1% as a default does no harm and is a reasonable habit, but it is not the meaningful variable people often treat it as.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do I need a decline function?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Only if you are training for a course with significant descent. Downhill running loads the quadriceps eccentrically, which is both the most effective way to prepare for a descent and the most effective way to make yourself very sore. It is genuinely valuable for that specific purpose and close to unused for anything else, so do not let it drive the buying decision.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How loud is a treadmill at running speed?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Expect a marked step up from walking. Most of the noise a downstairs neighbour hears is not the motor but footfall energy travelling through the frame into the floor, which is why heavier machines on a proper mat are quieter downstairs despite being louder beside the deck. If a shared floor is a concern, our quiet treadmill guide covers the distinction and what actually helps.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

